<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Constance</h1><p class="st-meta">1155 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Meersburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The temporal lordship of the bishops of Germany's largest medieval diocese, ruled from Meersburg after the Reformation drove them from Constance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a> became a bishopric around 600, serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni</a>, and grew into the largest diocese of the German lands, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. In 1155 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s possessions and rights, the basis of a small secular territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a> became an imperial city independent of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> from 1414 to 1418, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>. The bishops acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichenau-island">Reichenau</a> abbey in 1540. After Constance became Protestant in 1527, they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meersburg">Meersburg</a>, where they built a baroque New Palace.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the prince-bishopric was secularised and its lands went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. The diocese itself was dissolved in 1821, its parts divided between the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archdiocese-of-freiburg">Archdiocese of Freiburg</a> and Swiss dioceses.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-constance.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Constance" width="320" height="182"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Seutter, c. 1740-1750 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seutter_Bodensee_Constance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1155 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Meersburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hugo von Hohenlandenberg, Karl Theodor von Dalberg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Konstanz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1479&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-constance" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Constance on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4896 506 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Constance lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5216 762L5229 694M5216 762L5194 723M5216 762L5211 762"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5194 723h0M5211 762h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5216 762h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5216 762h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Meersburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1479&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-constance">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
